    "created_at": "2026-06-18T18:57:43Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-18T18:57:43Z",
    "body": "I'm one of the Temporal champions and have a few questions:\n  \n> **`timezone`**: string representing a timezone identifier, the string `\"input\"`, or the string `\"\"` (default). Controls what timezone to convert the provided datetime into for display.\n> * `\"input\"` indicates to use the calendar of the provided date time. `\"\"` indicates to use the UA's current timezone.\n\nLooks like a copy/paste mistake. I think you mean \"timezone of the provided date time\" not \"calendar...\". \n\nAlso, the `\"input\"` vs. `\"\"` choice is interesting. Another option could use the type of the input: if the input contains a time zone annotation like `[America/New_York]` then the time zone of the input could be used, otherwise it'd rely on the time zone of the user agent. Why did you decide against this option?\n\nRelated: if we do end up using `\"input\"`, then what will happen if `timezone=\"input\"` but the input lacks a time zone annotation?\n\nThe same questions apply to calendars, but with a twist: all non-`Instant` datetime types in Temporal have a calendar, but if the calendar is `iso8601` then no calendar annotation is shown. But given that we consider `iso8601` to be a \"null calendar\" for the purpose of localized formatting (meaning the UA's default calendar should be used), then the same \"if a calendar annotation is present then use it, otherwise use the UA's calendar\" option could apply as discussed above with time zones.\n\n> The attribute values map to ECMA-402 formatting options through the following JS algorithm:\n\nI didn't see the 12 vs. 24 hour format shown in the algorithm.\n\n> * **`hour12`**: boolean attribute with `\"\"` default. Controls whether 12 hour time or 24 hour time, or locale default are used.\n> * an attribute to switch between 24 and 12 hour time display (name TBD). Tri-state for 12, 24, or locale default.\n\nWhich of these two redundant bullet points are correct?\n\nAlso, why did you choose to use a boolean vs.  the [four choices of `hourCycle`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Intl/DateTimeFormat/DateTimeFormat#hourcycle)? Not saying this was a bad choice, but would be good to document the reasoning.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
