    "updated_at": "2026-06-23T18:57:21Z",
    "body": "@justingrant \n\n> Also, the \"input\" vs. \"\" choice is interesting. Another option could use the type of the input: if the input contains a time zone annotation like [America/New_York] then the time zone of the input could be used, otherwise it'd rely on the time zone of the user agent. Why did you decide against this option?\n\nBecause it would change the behaviour based on whether a user uses an `Instant` (converted to local time for display), or a `ZonedDateTime` (not converted to local time), or a plain date/time/datetime (which is assumed to be in local timezone for formatting). If the default is \"always display in local time\", then all three behave the same.\n\nI expect it to be common for `ZonedDateTime` to be used relatively interchangeably with `Instant`, with some users preferring `ZonedDateTime` for it's arithmetic capabilities rather than the fact that it is pinned to any time zone.\n\nMessage Format 2 independently came to the same conclusion about default behaviour here, which suggests to me that it is the correct one.\n\n> Related: if we do end up using \"input\", then what will happen if timezone=\"input\" but the input lacks a time zone annotation?\n\nFormatting fails and the fallback is used. It's like specifying an invalid timezone, or an invalid date time string, or an invalid option.\n\n> The same questions apply to calendars, but with a twist: all non-Instant datetime types in Temporal have a calendar, but if the calendar is iso8601 then no calendar annotation is shown. But given that we consider iso8601 to be a \"null calendar\" for the purpose of localized formatting (meaning the UA's default calendar should be used), then the same \"if a calendar annotation is present then use it, otherwise use the UA's calendar\" option could apply as discussed above with time zones.\n\nI was going to answer to that \"because iso8601 doesn't format usefully, so the default should not be that\", but I am sympathetic to your point regarding considering iso8601 to be \"null\" and thus defaulting it to UA calendar. That does pose a weird problem though: if you explicitly specify iso8601 as the calendar on your datetime, then it will still get defaulted to the UA calendar unless you specify `calendar=iso8601` explicitly.\n\nWe should think about this case some more. I see the following options:\n\n- Always convert to UA calendar, except when `calendar=input` or `calendar=<id>` is set.\n- Convert iso8601 to UA calendar, except when `calendar=<id>` is set. Needs an option to force to UA calendar?\n\nI still lean towards the first, because I think it is also what MF2 does. Also it is the least difficult to explain.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
